#: {{syn|en|level up}}
#: {{ux|en|I '''levelled''' after defeating the dragon.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[aim]] or [[direct]] (a [[weapon]], a [[stare]], an [[accusation]], etc).
#: {{ux|en|He '''levelled''' an accusation of fraud at the directors.&emsp; {{nowrap|The hunter '''levels''' the gun before taking a shot.}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1592|author=w:John Stow|title=The Annales of England
|passage=Bertram de Gordon, standing on the castle wall, '''levelled''' a quarrel out of a crossbow.}}
#* {{RQ:Jefferies Amateur Poacher|chapter=1|passage=But then I had the [massive] flintlock by me for protection. ¶{{...}}The linen-press and a chest on the top of it formed, however, a very good gun-carriage; and, thus mounted, aim could be taken out of the window&nbsp;[&hellip;], and a 'bead' could be drawn upon Molly, the dairymaid, kissing the fogger behind the hedge, little dreaming that the deadly tube was '''levelled''' at them.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|author=HarryBlank|work=w:SCP Foundation|title=Fire in the Hole|date=12 October 2023|url=https://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/35-fire-in-the-hole|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20240522030316/https://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/35-fire-in-the-hole|archivedate=22 May 2024|accessdate=27 May 2024|text=<p>The force of her horror must have been palpable in the air, because Jorge's eyes opened as she raised the bayonet up. "Fina?"</p><p>"How...?" she managed.</p><p>He sat bolt upright, and swung his feet over the bed. "Fina, it isn't safe here. You need to hide."</p><p>She kept the bayonet '''levelled''' on him. "I thought you were dead."</p>}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[direct]] or [[impose]] (a [[penalty]], [[fine]], etc) at or upon (someone) {{+obj|en|:on/:against/:at}}.
#: {{syn|en|levy}}
#* '''1809''', William Ross (Jr.), ''Abridgement of the laws of Scotland relating to hunting [etc]'', page 60:
#*: If the right of killing salmon belong exclusively to the King, and consequently to his donatories, why has not the Legislature secured the right by '''levelling''' penalties against such as should encroach upon it&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;?
#* '''1978''', ''Parliamentary Debates'' of the New Zealand House of Representatives, page 4955:
#*: How can the Minister reconcile the first statement with the clause, when he is in fact '''levelling''' punishment at the woman and not at the errant father&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;?
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1995|journal=The Parliamentary Debates (Hansard) of the [Great British] House of Lords
|passage=There is no purpose in '''levelling''' fines because they would be merely paid from the £1.8 billion which the BBC collects.}}
#* '''2007''', Mary Jacoby, ''EU investigators endorse charges against Intel'', Wall Street Journal Europe, 17 January, page 32, column 5:
#*: Ultimately, Ms. Kroes [European Union Antitrust Commissioner] could '''level''' a fine and order Intel to change its business practices.
# {{lb|en|intransitive|sports}} To make the [[score]] of a game equal.
